# bug-n-a-dynamically-dispatched-call-fills-its-defaults-from-another-class-signature
#
# A call on a dynamically-typed receiver dispatched at run time but took its
# PARAMETER DEFAULTS AND ARITY from whichever same-named method the candidate
# scan happened to see first. The scan is over the classes compiled SO FAR and
# was treated as exhaustive; a module pulled later declares classes it cannot
# see, and function-local imports make that the ordinary case.
#
# Found by attempting lekkerzeilen. wind.py imports nothing and __main__.py
# takes `from .wind import Wind` before `from . import world as world_mod`, so
# at wind.py parse time the only visible `.at` was Wind's. The compiler's own
# scan said so:
#
#   SCAN .at pick=Breeze nDual=4 arms=[TPyBytes TPyList TPyDeque TPyRange]
#
# -- the pick is the caller-module sibling and the class the receiver actually
# is was not even an arm. `Wind.at(self, x, z, t=0.0, height=6.0)` against
# `TiledGrid.at(self, x, z, outside=None)`: the call wrote two arguments, the
# site filled FOUR from Wind's signature, the bind against the three-parameter
# slot failed, and {VType=7, Payload=1} came back with no body run and no
# diagnostic -- to be dereferenced somewhere else entirely.
#
# THE DEFAULT VALUES ARE DELIBERATELY DISTINCT, not just differently counted.
# Grid's is 77.5 where Breeze's are 0.0 and 6.0, so a fill from the wrong
# signature cannot answer correctly by accident -- the failure mode this repo
# pays most for is a plausible wrong value, and an expected value that collides
# with the wrong one is a row that cannot fail.
#
# THE LAST TWO ROWS ARE THE CONTROL and they matter as much as the first two.
# Their receiver really IS the class the scan picked, so both must still take
# the STATIC arm -- which is what says the fix narrowed the change to calls
# whose arity the call site fabricated, rather than loosening dispatch
# generally.
#
# A third control -- a call written in FULL, filling no defaults -- is
# deliberately NOT here. It fails, on a SEPARATE defect this fix uncovered by
# making such calls complete at all
# (bug-n-a-run-time-dispatched-call-s-result-is-coerced-to-an-integer), and it
# is asserted in that ticket's own repro. A fixture red for a reason it is not
# about is a fixture nobody reads.
# The rows are printed from INSIDE the package, by a function the package
# exports. They have to be: the call sites must be compiled while early.py is
# the module being parsed, which is the condition the whole defect needs, and a
# call written out here would be compiled with both modules already pulled.
from nilpy_dynsig import run

run()
